# 0. An AI-oriented language

It's an interesting BOTH quest AND question: what an
AI-oriented programming language could look like? What properties it should
have? And where one goes with it when it comes to mixing knowledge, natural
language, with both structured and unstructured data. As well as evidence of
what is true and false, and how to reason about things in an almost precise
logical way.

More programs are written by a language model every year. A person states
what they want, an agent writes the program and runs it, and the person reads
the result. An **AI-oriented language** is a language designed for this
division of work: a model writes the program, a machine checks it, and a
person can still read it.

A model makes mistakes of its own kind. It misspells a field, calls a function
that does not exist, or edits a program until a test passes. It also corrects
a mistake well when a report says exactly what is wrong. A language for this
writer rejects as many wrong programs as it can, and reports each rejection
precisely.

HQL is built on three observations:

1. Agents write SQL well. A language for them queries and transforms
   structured data the way SQL does, and reads that data from the systems
   where it lives.
2. Agents need a checker. Static types, contracts on data and errors kept as
   values turn a mistake into a report the agent can act on.
3. Markdown is the format people and models already exchange. A document that
   holds text, queries and their results is a place where both can work.

SQL is the language of structured data, and Markdown is the language of
written knowledge. HQL joins the two: a typed query language whose programs
also live inside Markdown documents.

```d2
direction: down

person: "person\nstates the task, reads the result" {
  shape: person
}
agent: "agent\nwrites the program" {
  shape: page
}
check: "check\nevery type, before anything runs" {
  style.fill: "#eef6ee"
}
run: "run\nvalidate data against its contract" {
  style.fill: "#eef6ee"
}
values: "values\nresults and violations, both data" {
  shape: page
  style.fill: "#eef6ee"
}

person -> agent
agent -> check
check -> run: "the program fits its types"
run -> values
values -> person
check -> agent: "check error" {
  style.stroke: "#b23b3b"
  style.stroke-dash: 5
}
values -> agent: "violations" {
  style.stroke: "#b23b3b"
  style.stroke-dash: 5
}
```

Nothing here requires an agent. A person who writes HQL by hand gets the same
checks and the same reports. The rest of this chapter shows each observation
as a small program, and names the chapter that covers it.

| Property | What the writer gets | Covered in |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Query steps like SQL's | a familiar way to ask questions of data | [SQL-like Queries](06-sql-like-queries.hmd) |
| Static types | a wrong program is rejected before it runs | [The type system](10-the-type-system/index.hmd) |
| Contracts on data | bad data is rejected where it is read | [Advanced data typing](11-advanced-data-typing/index.hmd) |
| Errors as values | a failure can be queried and routed | [Errors](12-errors/index.hmd) |
| Declarations with a Rust body | short programs over tested implementations | [Functions and declarations](32-functions-and-declarations/index.hmd) |
| Queries inside documents | text, program and result in one file | [Queries inside documents](24-documents.hmd) |

## Queries

Models have read a great deal of SQL, and they write it well. HQL asks the
same questions as SQL. Each question is a step in a pipeline:

```hql
shelf = [
    {title: "Frankenstein", genre: "novel", pages: 280},
    {title: "Dune", genre: "novel", pages: 412},
    {title: "Leaves of Grass", genre: "poetry", pages: 145},
]
shelf
| filter(b => b.genre == "novel")
| map(b => b.title)
```

```text
["Frankenstein", "Dune"]
```

`filter` is `WHERE` and `map` is `SELECT`. A step takes one value and returns
another, so a program is read from top to bottom, and a step can be added or
removed without touching the others.

Data for a question is seldom in one place. Orders are in a table, corrections
arrive on a topic, and the reason for an exception is in a note. HQL reads
files, tables, topics and Markdown documents with the same steps, so an agent
writes one program where it would otherwise write a query, a script and the
code that joins their answers.
[[book#part-ii-data-flows|Part II]] covers these systems.

## A wrong program is rejected

`hql` checks the whole program before it runs any of it. Here a field name is
misspelt:

```hql
type Book = {title: String, pages: Int}

shelf = [
    Book(title = "Frankenstein", pages = 280),
    Book(title = "Dune", pages = 412),
]
shelf | map(b => b.page) | sum
```

```text
check error[unknown-field]: Book has no field `page`
 --> <expression>:7:18
  |
7 | shelf | map(b => b.page) | sum
  |                  ^^^^^^
```

Nothing ran. The report names the type, the field and the position. In a
language without this check the program would run and return a missing value
or a wrong total, and the agent would have to find the cause from the result.
With the report, the correction is one word:

```hql
type Book = {title: String, pages: Int}

shelf = [
    Book(title = "Frankenstein", pages = 280),
    Book(title = "Dune", pages = 412),
]
shelf | map(b => b.pages) | sum
```

```text
692
```

The same holds for a function that is not there. `median` is declared in the
module `stats`, and this program does not import it:

```hql
[280, 412, 145] | median
```

```text
check error[unknown-name]: `median` is declared in the module `stats`: write `from stats import median`
 --> <expression>:1:19
  |
1 | [280, 412, 145] | median
  |                   ^^^^^^
```

The report states the line to write. HQL has no implicit conversions and no
undefined behaviour: a value is never converted without a written step, a
`match` must cover every case, and a division by zero stops the run with a
report. [[01-hello-world|Chapter 1]] introduces the check, and
[[10-the-type-system|chapter 10]] covers the type system.

## Bad data is a value

The checker reads the program. It cannot read data that arrives while the
program runs. A **contract** covers that side. It is a type that states the
fields of a record, their types and the rules their values follow:

```hql
type BookData : DataContract = {
    title  : String
    pages  : Int
    status : String

    require pages > 0 as has_pages
    require ["todo", "reading", "done"] | contains(status) as known_status
}

shelf : List<Data> = [
    {title: "Dune", pages: 412, status: "reading"},
    {title: "The Hobbit", pages: 0, status: "lost"},
]

shelf
| validate(BookData)
| fork {
    clean    = oks()  | map(b => b.title),
    rejected = errs() | map(e => {title: e.value.title, rules: e.violations | map(v => v.rule)}),
}
```

```text
{clean: ["Dune"], rejected: [{rules: ["BookData.has_pages", "BookData.known_status"], title: "The Hobbit"}]}
```

`validate` checks each record against the contract and returns a result for
each: the typed record, or the record with its violations. It reports every
rule a record breaks, not only the first. `fork` sends the results to two
named branches. `oks()` keeps the accepted records and `errs()` keeps the
rejected ones.

The rejected side is a list of records like any other. A program can count
it, group it by rule, or write it to a table. An agent gets the failure as
data it can query, where an exception would give it a message to read.
[[11-advanced-data-typing|Chapter 11]] covers contracts and validation, and
[[12-errors|chapter 12]] covers errors.

## Rust underneath

A model writes a short program more reliably than a long one. HQL keeps
programs short by giving the agent functions that are already written and
tested:

```hql
from stats import median

[280, 412, 145] | median
```

```text
280.0
```

`median` has no body in HQL. It is a **declaration**: a name and a signature
whose result comes from a function written in Rust. The checker types a call
from the signature alone, so a declaration is called and checked like any
other function. The standard library is built this way, and a package can add
declarations of its own.

Rust suits this role for the reasons this chapter gives for HQL. It is
statically typed, it handles errors as values, and safe Rust has no undefined
behaviour. The agent writes the high-level program in HQL, and the heavy
operations run in Rust that was tested once.
[[book#part-v-extending-hql|Part V]] covers declarations, extensions and
packages.

## Markdown as the medium

People write notes, specifications and instructions for agents in Markdown,
and models answer in it. In HQL a fenced `hql` block in a Markdown document is
a **cell**, a program that `hql render` runs:

````markdown
## Still to read

```hql
cards
| filter(c => c.metadata.status == "todo")
| map(c => c.title)
```
````

`hql render` writes the document with the cell's result in it. The text, the
query and the result are in one file. The file is plain Markdown: it opens in
any editor, and a diff shows what changed. [[24-documents|Chapter 24]] covers
cells, and [[21-a-vault|chapter 21]] covers the vault of documents they query.

## A person in the loop

!!! warning "Design only"

    The `ai` module is not in the current release.
    [[25-agents-and-runs|Chapters 25]] to [[27-the-hyper-web|27]] describe
    its design.

A chat session keeps its context in one growing prompt. A person cannot easily
see what the model was given, or change one part of it and run it again.

In a vault the context is a set of documents. A card links to other cards and
embeds parts of them. A cell selects the cards a question needs. The prompt is
the result of evaluating these cards, and each part of it is a file. An agent
is a card too: its body is its instructions, and its header lists its model
and its tools. A run is a value that a later program can read.

This gives the person three things:

- **The context is explicit.** Every piece of text the model received is in a
  card or is the output of a cell.
- **Every part can be inspected and changed.** A person opens a card, edits
  its instructions or its query, and renders that part again.
- **Changes are versioned.** The cards are files, so the history of a prompt
  is the history of the files.

An agent can edit a query or write a new card, and the cells that depend on
it are evaluated again. The person checks the change in the same files.

